  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nRain showers continue to be sporadic this morning across \neastern New York and western New England as a weather system is \nmoving through. The probabilities for precipitation have been \nadjusted for low to medium chances through 6 to 9 AM this \nmorning. Then, precipitation chances remain through the mid-\nafternoon across the high terrain locations of southern Vermont,\nBerkshires, Taconics, and eastern Catskills as moisture is \nstill present at these locations for light rain shower activity \nto continue. There are no flooding concerns for today as the \nnature of showers is light. There are low chances (less than 30 \npercent) for a non- severe thunderstorm in the Mid-Hudson Valley\nand Litchfield Hills from 2 PM to 8 PM this evening. The most \nlikely scenario supported by the latest forecast is a rumble of \nthunder and scattered rain showers during this timeframe. Once \nthe sun sets this evening, dry conditions are in store for \neastern New York and western New England until tomorrow. There \ncould be some patchy fog late tonight into tomorrow morning, but\nconfidence is low on this potential due to how long the clouds \nstick around during the overnight hours. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nFor tomorrow, there are low to medium chances for light rain \nshowers across portions of eastern New York and western New \nEngland. As rain shower activity will be light and sporadic \nthroughout the day, there are no flooding concerns. The only \nlocations that will likely not see a rain shower tomorrow is in \nthe southern Adirondacks, Lake George-Saratoga region, and \nsouthern Vermont. This is primarily due to a weather system \nmoving further south and east tomorrow for the chances of \nprecipitation to be for locations from the Mohawk Valley, \nGreater Capital District, Berkshires, and southward. The \ncurrent forecast supports timing of rain shower activity to be \nin the mid- morning to late afternoon hours from north to \nsouth. Then, dry conditions are in store for Friday night into \nearly Saturday morning before our next chances of precipitation\nreturn for Saturday afternoon. \n\nFor Saturday afternoon and evening, an upper level shortwave is\nsupported by latest forecast model guidances to move through \neastern New York and western New England for one more day of \nunsettled weather conditions. While most locations will see \npartly to mostly cloudy skies, there are medium chances (40-60%)\nfor rain showers and a rumble of thunder for southern Vermont \nand the Berkshires during the afternoon hours Saturday. There \nare low chances (less than 30 percent) across the southern \nAdirondacks, Mohawk Valley, Schoharie Valley, Greater Capital \nDistrict, and Lake George-Saratoga regions. With the nature of \nthe rain showers to be on the light side, there are no flooding \nconcerns for Saturday afternoon and evening. Then, surface high \npressure builds in from the northwest and dry conditions return \nfor the rest of the Labor Day weekend across eastern New York \nand western New England.",
